============================================================
7. HOME SCREEN
============================================================

The Home screen should immediately answer:

"What matters to a BK Häcken men's supporter right now?"

Show, in a clear order:

1. Next men's match
   - opponent
   - competition
   - date
   - time if available
   - home/away

2. Warning/suspension situation
   - suspended for next match
   - one warning from suspension
   - other relevant warning information

3. Latest result
   - opponent
   - score
   - competition
   - date

4. Recent relevant men's news
   - approximately 3–5 items
   - title
   - date
   - source/category as appropriate

5. League table/position if reliable data is available.

Do not create a large hero image.

Do not use a carousel.

Do not fill the screen with decorative cards.

The next match and warning status should be visually prominent.

============================================================
8. MATCHES
============================================================

The Matches area must show men's first-team matches.

For each match support, where data is available:

- date
- competition
- opponent
- home/away
- result
- score
- goals
- cards
- starting XI
- substitutions
- player minutes
- assists
- other relevant player-level statistics available from the chosen source

For the latest completed match, make player statistics particularly useful.

A simple table/list is preferable to a visually overloaded football dashboard.

Example conceptual structure:

Player | Minutes | Goals | Assists | Yellow

Adapt to the actual data returned by the source.

Do not display fields merely because the API provides them.

============================================================
9. WARNING / SUSPENSION FEATURE
============================================================

This is a core feature.

The app must not simply display:

"Player has 2 yellow cards."

Instead it must answer:

- Who is suspended for the next match?
- Who is one warning away from suspension?
- Which competition/season does the warning status refer to?

Model warnings by:

- player
- competition
- season
- match history
- warnings
- suspension state

Do not mix warnings across competitions.

Do not apply a generic threshold blindly.

Use the current applicable SvFF competition regulations for the relevant competition/season and document the rule in the repository.

For the 2026 competition rules, SvFF's regulations specify that a player who receives three warnings in different matches in the same competition serves a suspension in the same competition. Verify the exact applicable rule during implementation and model it explicitly rather than hard-coding an undocumented assumption.

The warning logic must be unit tested.

Acceptance examples must include:

- player with two relevant warnings and no suspension -> appears in "En varning från avstängning"
- player who has reached the applicable threshold -> appears as suspended when the suspension applies
- warnings from another competition do not incorrectly affect the Allsvenskan status
- season boundaries do not incorrectly carry old warnings into a new season
- match-specific suspension status is correctly associated with the next relevant match

Use actual source data and rule configuration rather than hand-coded player-specific exceptions.

============================================================
10. BK HÄCKEN NEWS
============================================================

Current men's team news is a core feature.

Do not simply filter articles by the presence of the word "Herr".

BK Häcken's site can contain:
- men's articles
- women's articles
- academy/youth material
- club-wide material
- mixed tags
- articles whose relevance is not perfectly represented by one tag

Build a robust relevance/classification pipeline.

Primary source:

BK Häcken's official news/press ecosystem.

Use its available RSS feeds where appropriate instead of unnecessarily scraping HTML.

Investigate the available official feeds during implementation.

The repository should document exactly which feed(s) are being used.

Relevant official BK Häcken news should be classified into:

- men
- women
- youth/academy
- club/general
- unknown

Only relevant men's content should appear in the men's news view, while club-wide content may be included when genuinely relevant.

Do not invent a classification.

If deterministic rules are insufficient, use conservative classification and/or LLM classification only where justified.

============================================================
11. FORMER BK HÄCKEN PLAYERS
============================================================

This is the second major feature.

The user wants to follow former BK Häcken players.

This does NOT mean showing their latest match as if they were still BK Häcken players.

Instead it is a career-monitoring feature.

For a former player, show where relevant:

- player name
- current club
- current league/country
- current-season statistics
- appearances
- starts
- minutes
- goals
- assists
- cards where useful
- contract expiry/status if supported
- transfers
- loans
- contract extensions
- relevant career news

Use factual playing-volume indicators.

Do NOT show subjective statements such as:

- "in poor form"
- "ordinary season"
- "looks ready for a return"
- "should return to Häcken"

unless the user explicitly asks for analysis later.

Do not infer transfer likelihood.

============================================================
12. FORMER PLAYER FAVORITES
============================================================

Allow users to mark former BK Häcken players as favorites.

Favorites must persist locally across sessions.

Use localStorage or another appropriate browser-local mechanism.

Do not require login.

Do not require an account.

Do not require a backend.

The favorite list must be easy to modify.

The UI should make it obvious which players are currently followed.

============================================================
13. FORMER PLAYER CONTRACT DATA
============================================================

Contract expiry is important.

But this is also a high-risk data field.

NEVER display an unverified contract date as a confirmed fact.

Every contract claim must carry provenance.

Use a model conceptually like:

{
  player,
  contractStatus,
  contractExpiry,
  verificationStatus,
  source,
  sourceUrl,
  publishedAt,
  discoveredVia,
  retrievedAt
}

Possible verification states:

- confirmed
- reported
- unverified
- unknown

Examples:

CONFIRMED:
"Contract expires December 2026"
only when supported by an appropriate source.

REPORTED:
"Contract reportedly runs until December 2026"
when a reputable source reports it but primary confirmation is unavailable.

UNKNOWN:
"No verified contract expiry found."

Never turn an uncertain date into a hard date merely because a database contains one.

============================================================
14. FORMER PLAYER NEWS RESEARCH
============================================================

This is where web search/research is useful.

The system should be capable of finding relevant news about followed former players.

Relevant categories include:

- transfer
- loan
- contract extension
- contract expiry
- contract negotiations
- new club
- departure
- return
- major career development
- relevant injury/comeback where genuinely newsworthy
- significant football career events

Do not search for every player continuously at high frequency.

Use the user's favorite list to constrain monitoring.

============================================================
